WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:05.623
- Hi, my name's Alon Shaya, and
today, we're talking hummus.

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:09.830
My favorite is kinda like the
classic hummus with tahini,

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.123
which we're doing today,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.333
but my rule of thumb is
everything goes good on hummus.

00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:18.180
So, the baking soda really
begins to break down

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.970
the structure of the skins

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.710
that are on top of the garbanzo beans.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:22.740
When you soak them in it,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.010
it starts to break it down a little bit,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.990
but then when you drain it
and bake it with a lot more,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.100
it really dries on there and
really starts to break down

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.270
all that cell structure of the skins.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.010
And so, now, when we go to rinse it,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.980
those skins will just
kind of start to fall off.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.060
I'm kind of massaging the beans to get the

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.483
baking soda off of it,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.810
but also to get the skins
to start coming off,

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.150
and that's what's gonna
really make it nice and tender

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.943
is as those skins fall off,
you get a very smooth puree.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.473
The hummus was one of the
major major things for us.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.600
We made, I think, 80
batches before we got it

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:00.660
right where we wanted it.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.230
If we didn't get the hummus right,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.180
we wouldn't be standing here right now.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.653
You want 'em to be falling
apart, super mushy.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:13.938
When you sit down in an
Arab restaurant in Israel,

00:01:13.938 --> 00:01:16.290
you typically get plates of hummus

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.650
and then you get plates
of Moroccan carrot salad

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.080
and pickled cauliflower and
tabouleh and baba ghanoush

00:01:24.206 --> 00:01:27.720
and tahini and maybe some grated tomato

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.370
and extra virgin olive
oil and a little sea salt

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:34.043
and spicy peppers, and
then you get all this pita,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.630
and then that's kinda
the way your meal starts.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.710
And we were very particular
to not cut the bread

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.290
into little triangles or whatever,
or serve it with a knife.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.593
We wanted people to grab it, tear it open,

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.367
burn their fingers, and then
feel that kind of connection

00:01:54.617 --> 00:01:57.000
where you're passing
somebody a piece of bread

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.100
and then you're grabbing a plate

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.080
and then you're sharing this

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.283
and you're making bites for people.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:03.870
That's really kind of the beauty

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.433
of the food that we cook, I think.

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:09.152
(upbeat music)

